# Models are chosen on the setup screen, not here — it lists what the backend
# below has installed and preselects that backend's recommendations (see
# BACKENDS in config.py), downloading them when it can. Every model runs
# locally, so nothing here needs an API key unless you gave your own server one.

# Which inference server answers. Backends are registered in
# adapters/outbound/llm_backends.py:
#   lmstudio — LM Studio's local server, http://localhost:1234/v1. Serves no
#              /rerank route at all, so a reranker there means RERANK_* below.
#   vmlx     — vMLX on Apple Silicon, http://localhost:8000/v1, started with
#              `vmlx serve <chat-model> --embedding-model <embedder>`. It does
#              serve /v1/rerank, which is the reason to reach for it.
#   llamacpp — llama.cpp's own server, http://localhost:1235/v1, started with
#              `llama-server -hf <repo> --rerank --port 1235`. The engine LM
#              Studio embeds, run as the HTTP server LM Studio does not expose,
#              so /v1/rerank is reachable. One process holds one model, which
#              is why this is normally a RERANK_BACKEND rather than an
#              LLM_BACKEND.
LLM_BACKEND=lmstudio

# Where that backend is answering. Unset means the address registered for the
# backend above, which is a localhost one — the app and the server side by side.
# This is the single setting that moves inference to another machine, and
# "another machine" covers the loopback case too: there is no separate mode.
#   LLM_BASE_URL=http://ai-01.internal:8000/v1   # its own box
#   LLM_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8000/v1  # app in Docker, vMLX on the host
# LLM_BASE_URL=

# ── Server mode ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Naming all three of these answers everything the setup screen asks, so an app
# started by a process manager comes up ready instead of waiting for a click.
# It also hands model lifecycle to whoever runs the inference server: the app
# stops downloading, loading and unloading. Leave them unset for the ordinary
# interactive case, where the setup screen chooses.
#
# EMBED_MODEL names the vector collection, so every deployment sharing an index
# must spell it identically — see docs/DEPLOY.md.
# CHAT_MODEL=
# OCR_MODEL=
# EMBED_MODEL=

# The two optional models. Unset falls back to the backend's recommendation;
# `none` is how you decline one it recommends.
# RERANKER_MODEL=none
# JUDGE_MODEL=none

# CHUNKING_STRATEGY=recursive

# Where the PDFs to ingest are read from. Running locally this is a folder on
# this machine; the deployed image already fixes it at /data/documents, so leave
# it alone there and set DOCUMENTS_HOST_DIR instead — docker-compose.yml reads
# that one to decide which host folder to mount. Two names because they are two
# paths, and one name for both breaks as soon as the file is passed through to
# the container.
# DOCUMENTS_DIR=
# DOCUMENTS_HOST_DIR=/srv/pdfs

# Reranking alone, when the backend above cannot do it. Either setting on its
# own is enough: a bare RERANK_BASE_URL is a second server of the same kind, a
# bare RERANK_BACKEND is a different kind at its own default address. Unset —
# the default — and one server answers for all four capabilities.
# The pairing this exists for — LM Studio answering everything except the one
# route it has none of, with a llama.cpp beside it holding a cross-encoder:
# RERANK_BACKEND=llamacpp
# RERANK_BASE_URL=http://localhost:1235/v1

# Bearer token for a server started with `--api-key`. Neither authenticates
# anything until it is given one, so both stay unset in the ordinary case.
# VMLX_API_KEY=
# LLAMA_API_KEY=

# Vector store. Backends are registered in adapters/outbound/vector_stores.py:
#   milvus — VECTOR_URI is a local milvus-lite file, or http://host:19530
#   memory — no dependencies, no persistence; VECTOR_URI is ignored
VECTOR_BACKEND=milvus
VECTOR_URI=chatbot.db
VECTOR_COLLECTION=documents

# Document catalog: one SQLite row per document seen, holding the title its
# citations are shown under. Outlives the vector store, which every ingest
# rebuilds from scratch.
CATALOG_URI=catalog.db

# Interaction log: one row per answered question, with the searches it ran, the
# passages they returned, which of those the answer cited, and what it all cost.
# Written on every answer and read by the Statistics page. Append-only — deleting
# this file loses the history and nothing else.
ANALYTICS_URI=analytics.db

# Answer cache. Unset and there is none: every question is answered from the
# corpus, which is what this app did before the cache existed. Set it and a
# question close enough to one already answered comes back from Redis without a
# model call. `docker compose` sets it for you; this is the address for a Redis
# you are running yourself.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# Minimum cosine similarity to serve a stored answer, and how long one may be
# served for. The threshold is strict on purpose — too loose and the cache
# answers a question nobody asked. Raise it from what the Statistics page
# reports for real misses rather than lowering it on a hunch.
# ANSWER_CACHE_THRESHOLD=0.92
# ANSWER_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=3600

# ── Prompts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Where the prompts this app sends to a model are read from. Unset — the
# default — and they are the constants in `domain/constants.py`: the system
# prompt, the search tool's description, the OCR instruction and the two the
# judge uses. Nothing is asked of anything, and this app behaves exactly as it
# did before a registry existed.
#
# Set it and each of those five is read from the `prompt-registry` service
# instead, by key, so a prompt can be edited and published in the registry's
# console and picked up by a running app without a restart or a redeploy. The
# keys are namespaced under `chatbot.`:
#
#   chatbot.system            the system prompt
#   chatbot.search-tool       the search tool's description
#   chatbot.ocr-instruction   what a vision model is told to do with a page
#   chatbot.judge             the judge's system prompt
#   chatbot.judge-request     the judge's request template
#
# Any key the registry has no published version for falls back to the constant,
# per key, so publishing one of them and leaving the rest alone is a valid way
# to run this. So is the registry being down: a lookup that fails is the
# built-in prompt, never a failed answer.
#
# One thing a registered system prompt must do. Write the two refusal sentences
# as `{{refusal_sentence}}` and `{{partial_refusal_sentence}}` rather than
# spelling them out — this app recognises a refusal by matching them, and a
# reworded copy would be read as an ordinary answer that cited nothing. See
# `domain/constants.py`.
# PROMPT_REGISTRY_URL=http://localhost:8080

# Bearer token, if the registry sits behind something that authenticates. The
# registry's own API has none of its own.
# PROMPT_REGISTRY_TOKEN=

# How long a resolved prompt is reused before the registry is asked again. This
# is both the lag between publishing a prompt and a running app using it, and
# what keeps an HTTP call off the answer path. Five minutes by default.
# PROMPT_REGISTRY_TTL_SECONDS=300
